<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Leeward Islands colony</h1><p class="st-meta">1671 CE – 1958 CE · capital St John's</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Umbrella colony that grouped Britain's northern Lesser Antilles under one governor for nearly three centuries, from Antigua to the Virgin Islands.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1671 the crown separated the Leeward Caribee Islands from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/barbados">Barbados</a> and gave them their own governor-in-chief, first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-wheler">Sir Charles Wheler</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-stapleton-1st-baronet">Sir William Stapleton</a>. The group comprised <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-antigua">Antigua</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-saint-christopher">Saint Kitts</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-nevis">Nevis</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/montserrat">Montserrat</a>, later joined by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbuda">Barbuda</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anguilla">Anguilla</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/british-virgin-islands">British Virgin Islands</a>. A general legislature met occasionally from the 1680s.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Each island kept its own assembly and lieutenant governor, while the governor-in-chief, usually based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-johns-antigua-and-barbuda">St John's</a> on <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-antigua">Antigua</a>, handled defence and relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. Between 1816 and 1833 the islands were split into two governments. In 1871 they became a formal federal colony with a single legislature, which included <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/commonwealth-of-dominica">Dominica</a> until 1940. Their economies rested on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>, and later on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and small farming.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The federal colony was dissolved in 1956, and its members joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/west-indies-federation">West Indies Federation</a> in 1958, though the shared governorship lingered briefly. Its former members became <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/antigua-and-barbuda">Antigua and Barbuda</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/saint-kitts-and-nevis">Saint Kitts and Nevis</a>, and the territories of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/montserrat">Montserrat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anguilla">Anguilla</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/british-virgin-islands">British Virgin Islands</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/leeward-islands-colony.jpg" alt="Leeward Islands colony" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Cypp0847 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Leeward_Islands_(1871%E2%80%931952).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1671 CE – 1958 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>St John's</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sir William Stapleton, Christopher Codrington</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Anglicanism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/antigua-and-barbuda">Antigua and Barbuda</a>, <a href="/countries/saint-kitts-and-nevis">Saint Kitts and Nevis</a>, Montserrat, British Virgin Islands, <a href="/countries/anguilla">Anguilla</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>British Leeward Islands, Federal Colony of the Leeward Islands</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1814&amp;focus=leeward-islands-colony" aria-label="Leeward Islands colony on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3000 1543 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Leeward Islands colony lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000"